- **MPSCALC-314** (landing comment; MOVE candidate; `[REC-LAND-314-20NOV]`): `BLUF. MPSCALC-314: the minimum gap between autosave requests on the quote screen landed as MPS #223 on main after one independent review (go with findings at head b26cfc21); read at code main <<MAIN8>>, the one clause of this ticket's acceptance check reads met on the coordinator's reading, with limits that stay open. <<OUTCOME>> Records note, <<READ-DATE>>, read at code main <<MAIN8>>. Authority: no ruling of the product owner rests on this note; the ticket says the choice of fix is the coordinator's decision, and the choice made is the coordinator's default under the ticket's own text: a minimum gap, one named constant, one line to change. Landing: MPS #223, squash f8ac826621e6ce9403e986f1384d1abf8953cb25, merged 2026-11-20 19:34:00 AEDT (from 2026-11-20T08:34:00Z, by program), one parent debb5e28; 4 paths, 207 lines added and 5 removed: web/src/screens/quote/autosave.ts (+8), web/src/screens/QuoteScreen.tsx (+41 -3), web/src/screens/quote/autosave.test.ts (+9 -1) and web/src/screens/QuoteScreen.autosave.test.tsx (+149 -1). The squash tree equals the tree of the reviewed head b26cfc21, which is main plus one commit, and the hosted checks of that head read 9 of 9 success. What it does (file:line at code main <<MAIN8>>): web/src/screens/quote/autosave.ts:17 adds AUTOSAVE_MIN_GAP_MS (10 seconds) beside AUTOSAVE_QUIET_MS (:9, 2 seconds); web/src/screens/QuoteScreen.tsx:240 armGap starts the gap at every save request and is called at :268 before the request is sent; a change made inside the gap is sent by one trailing save when the gap ends (:246 to :249); the first save of a visit, leaving the screen and the explicit buttons (Save draft, Save and calculate) still send at once; the quiet interval, the server and the screen's words are unchanged. Review (relayed from its verdict record, read whole): go with findings at head b26cfc21 on main debb5e28, no Blocker, no Major, three Minor and six Note. The review reproduced the count this ticket is about by a program: with the constant at zero the ticket's minute of typing in bursts with 2500 millisecond pauses sent 22 requests, with the gap 6 by 60 seconds and 7 by 62 seconds; its attack on the behaviour found no way to lose a change. Its Minor findings: the builder strengthened a test cell that was blind as written instead of stopping, which the review accepted; two behaviours of the gap that no owned test pins (an explicit save starts a gap of its own, and a new request restarts a running gap); and a refused save starts a gap, so the next edit is held up to 10 seconds with the refusal message still on the screen (before: 2 seconds), after which it is sent and the message clears. Acceptance check (one sentence: a counting fake fails above one save request per quiet interval): MET on the coordinator's reading, which reads the sentence per gap as the build does: the test counts the saves in a 60 second run with 2500 millisecond pauses and fails above 6; one save per 10 seconds is at most one per 2 second quiet interval for autosave requests from one open screen; the ticket's other option, the server coalescing the history events, is not built and is not needed for this reading. The limits, in plain words (the review's list): the gap limits autosave requests from one open quote screen only, not saves from other screens, other browser tabs or other people, and not explicit saves; the gap is kept by the open screen, so a quote left and opened again inside the 10 seconds starts with the gap open; a change made inside the gap stays unsaved for up to 10 seconds unless the user leaves the screen or saves, the screen reads Unsaved changes for that time and the unsaved-changes prompt stays armed; and the exposure on an unclean exit (a crash, a discarded tab, a power loss) grew from the quiet interval of 2 seconds to the gap of 10 seconds. The other place that saves a draft, web/src/proposal/ContentEditor.tsx, is reached by a button only and is not changed. Not run by the review: a real browser tab closed or crashed inside the gap, and the hosted checks of the squash (see below). Done rule (entry C-79): Done means merged to main, the same as Composer, with each Done-when clause met; as the coordinator reads it, no review line or red run may stand against the move. The review's own note that a merge does not move the ticket rests on the older sentence that C-79 replaced. Hosted: this change is on main only and is NOT on the hosted site: the site holds main debb5e28 since the swap of 2026-11-20 (entry C-<<E188>>), which came before this squash, and putting any later main on the site needs a decision card. Hosted checks of the squash: <<CHECKS-223>>. <<FOLLOWUPS-314>> Recorded as entry C-<<E189>> in the project's clarifications record. [REC-LAND-314-20NOV]`
- **MPSCALC-418** (landing comment; MOVE candidate; `[REC-LAND-418-20NOV]`): `BLUF. MPSCALC-418: the infrastructure test now pins the value of every computed app setting and of the literals it did not pin; it landed as MPS #224 on main after one independent review (go with findings at head 2bf1d822); read at code main <<MAIN8>>, every clause of this ticket's acceptance check reads met on the coordinator's reading. <<OUTCOME>> Records note, <<READ-DATE>>, read at code main <<MAIN8>>. Authority: no ruling of the product owner rests on this note and the review says none is used or needed; no word of the product changes and nothing in this change reaches a customer. Landing: MPS #224, squash 28d8d31b73c6475b33921c679499b0777fdf0448, merged 2026-11-20 19:34:46 AEDT (from 2026-11-20T08:34:46Z, by program), one parent f8ac8266 (the squash of MPS #223, which landed 46 seconds earlier); 1 path, 145 lines added and 0 removed: infra/tests/test_infra.py (blob c0e6a272, equal to the blob of the reviewed head); no byte of the template, of the committed list of setting names or of any other test changed. Because MPS #223 landed first, the squash tree is the tree of MPS #223 plus this one file and is not the tree of the reviewed head, which was made on main debb5e28; the one path is the same. The hosted checks of the reviewed head read 9 of 9 success. What it does (file:line at code main <<MAIN8>>): infra/tests/test_infra.py:247 COMPUTED_SETTINGS pins the compiled expression string of six computed settings (the instance, the tenant, the two sign-in ids, the audience and the scope); the allowed hosts are pinned as a shape that leaves the site API version free (:256); :259 PINNED_LITERALS pins four literals no other check pinned; :265 MODULE_ID_PARAMS pins the two ids as the module call passes them; with these all 11 literals of the template are pinned (7 before, 4 now). Review (relayed from its verdict record, read whole): go with findings at head 2bf1d822 on main debb5e28, no Blocker, no Major, two Minor and three Note. Its measurements: 35 tests became 43; the build equals the reference diff of its kit; 13 of 13 planted changes of the template source turn the new guard red naming the key, and 10 of the 13 were invisible to the old file; no realistic wrong value stayed green among the 24 planted changes it ran. Its Minor findings: a loop over a table that can be empty cannot fail while the table is empty (only the planted-source test turns red when a table is emptied, and emptying the list of plants alone leaves the file green), and when the template has one real defect the whole set of mutant tests also turns red, so the first failure printed names another key or is a bare KeyError, although the key is named in every case. Acceptance check, clause by clause (the coordinator's reading, from the review's list): the test fails when the compiled value of any of the seven computed settings differs from the committed shape, shown by a planted change for each: MET; it fails on a changed literal that was not pinned: MET; it still passes on the real template: MET (43 tests); the count of tests is printed before and after: MET (35 and 43); no id is written to any file: MET (the review's scan found none); no other byte of infra changes: MET (the neighbour blobs are equal). Reading that differs from the ticket's words: the compiler never substitutes an id, so build with two synthetic ids became pin the exact expression string that Azure receives; the ticket's 10 shared literals read 11 in the template and the build follows the template. Left on the ticket, in the review's words: the seven site properties are not pinned (a follow-up), the hosted site itself cannot be seen by this file, renaming the template variable that the allowed-hosts pin names turns that pin red, and a compiler release that prints these expressions differently would do the same; only one compiler version was available to the review. Done rule (entry C-79): Done means merged to main,